ena: fix XDP multi-buffer being silently disabled on recent kernels

The ENA_HAVE_XDP_MB_DEPS compile probe tests for xdp_update_skb_shared_info(),
which upstream Linux renamed to xdp_update_skb_frags_info(). On kernels that
carry only the new name the probe fails, so ENA_HAVE_XDP_MB_DEPS and in turn
ENA_XDP_MB_SUPPORT are never defined. ena_get_max_xdp_mtu() then falls into its
"#ifndef ENA_XDP_MB_SUPPORT" branch and applies ENA_XDP_MAX_SINGLE_FRAME_SIZE
(3502 bytes) unconditionally, so a program's BPF_F_XDP_HAS_FRAGS flag can no
longer influence the MTU decision and XDP cannot attach at EC2's default MTU
of 9001:

  ena 0000:00:05.0 ens5: Failed to set xdp program, the current MTU (9001) is
  larger than the maximum allowed MTU (3502) while xdp is on

The driver body already uses the current name -- kcompat.h aliases
xdp_update_skb_frags_info back to xdp_update_skb_shared_info when
ENA_HAVE_XDP_FRAGS_INFO_AND_FLAGS is undefined, and the probe for that macro
later in the same file already tests the new name. Only this aggregate
dependency probe was missed.

Drop the call rather than renaming it: the remaining symbols already establish
multi-buffer capability, and renaming would break older kernels that carry only
the old name.

Reported upstream: https://github.com/amzn/amzn-drivers
Verified on c7gn.xlarge, Amazon Linux 2023, kernel 6.18.38-76.139, ena 2.17.2g.

diff --git a/kernel/linux/ena/config/test_defs.sh b/kernel/linux/ena/config/test_defs.sh
index dbc13f5..62b6e76 100755
--- a/kernel/linux/ena/config/test_defs.sh
+++ b/kernel/linux/ena/config/test_defs.sh
@@ -170,7 +170,6 @@ try_compile_async "#include <net/xdp.h>
                      xdp_get_shared_info_from_frame(NULL);
                      xdp_buff_has_frags(NULL);
                      xdp_frame_has_frags(NULL);
-                     xdp_update_skb_shared_info(NULL, 0, 0, 0, false);
                      xdp_buff_set_frags_flag(NULL);
                      __xdp_rxq_info_reg(NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 0);
                    }"                                                     \
